===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|id=hue|:inh|enm:blew}}

From {{inh|en|enm|blew,blewe}}, from {{der|en|xno|blew}},<ref>https://www.oed.com/view/Entry/20577</ref> from {{der|en|frm|bleu}}, from {{der|en|fro|bloe|blöe}}, {{m|fro|bleve}}, {{m|fro|blef|t=blue}}, from {{der|en|frk|*blāu|t=blue}} (perhaps through a {{der|en|la-lat|blāvus}}, {{m|la|blāvius||blue}} attested from Isidore of Seville), from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*blēwaz||blue, dark blue}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰlēw-||yellow, blond, grey}}. May have displaced native {{ncog|ang|hǣwen||blue}}.

Cognate with dialectal English {{m|en|blow||blue}}, {{cog|sco|blue}}, {{m|sco|blew||blue}}, {{cog|frr|bla}}, {{m|frr|blö||blue}}, {{cog|stq|blau||blue}}, {{cog|nl|blauw||blue}}, {{cog|de|blau||blue}}, {{cog|da,nb,nn,sv|blå||blue}}, {{cog|fo|bláur||blue}}, {{cog|is|blár||blue}}, {{cog|la|flāvus||yellow}}, {{cog|fr|bleu||blue}}, {{cog|mga|blá||yellow}}. Doublet of {{l|en|blow|id=blue}}.

Possibly related also to {{m+|en|blee|t=colour}}, from {{cog|ang|blēo|t=colour}}; but direct derivatives of {{inh|en|gem-pro|*blēwaz||blue, dark blue}} in Old English include: {{cog|ang|blāw}} and {{m|ang|blēo|t=blue}} (both only dubiously attested in compounds and place names), {{cog|ang|blǣwen|t=dark blue}}, {{m|ang|blǣhǣwen|t=blue-coloured, bluish, violet or purple colour, dark blue}}. There seems to be a parallel connection in Germanic between words for ''blue'' and ''colour'', dually exemplified by {{m+|gmw-pro|*blīu|t=colour, blee}} and {{m|gmw-pro|*blāu|t=blue}}; and {{ncog|gem-pro|*hiwją|t=colour, hue}} and {{m|gmw-pro|*hāwī|t=blue, purple}}.

{{sense|depressed}} Compare typologically {{m+|ru|тоска́ зелёная}} (<+ {{m|ru|зелёный}}). Also compare {{m|ru|фиоле́тово}}.

